According to 14 CFR § 107.31(a), with vision that is unaided by any device other than corrective lenses, the remote pilot in command, the person manipulating the flight controls, and the visual observer (if one is used) must be able to see the unmanned aircraft throughout the entire flight in order to:

1. Know the unmanned aircraft's location;
2. Determine the unmanned aircraft's attitude, altitude, and direction of flight;
3. Observe the airspace for other air traffic or hazards; and
4. Determine that the unmanned aircraft does not endanger the life or property of another (14 CFR § 107.31(a)).

According to 14 CFR § 107.73, the initial aeronautical knowledge test covers the following areas of knowledge:

(a) Applicable regulations relating to small unmanned aircraft system rating privileges, limitations, and flight operation;
(b) Airspace classification, operating requirements, and flight restrictions affecting small unmanned aircraft operation;
(c) Aviation weather sources and effects of weather on small unmanned aircraft performance;
(d) Small unmanned aircraft loading;
(e) Emergency procedures;
(f) Crew resource management;
(g) Radio communication procedures;
(h) Determining the performance of the small unmanned aircraft;
(i) Physiological effects of drugs and alcohol;
(j) Aeronautical decision-making and judgment;
(k) Airport operations;
(l) Maintenance and preflight inspection procedures; and
(m) Operation at night.

To take the knowledge test, an applicant must have proper identification at the time of application that contains the following:
1. A photograph (14 CFR § 107.67(b)(1));
2. A signature (14 CFR § 107.67(b)(2));
3. A date of birth, which shows the applicant meets or will meet the age requirements of the part for the certificate and rating sought before the expiration date of the airman knowledge test report (14 CFR § 107.67(b)(3)); and
4. A permanent mailing address (if the permanent mailing address is a post office box number, the applicant must also provide a current residential address) (14 CFR § 107.67(b)(4)). 

Additionally, FAA AC 107-2A specifies that the applicant must provide proper government-issued photo identification to the Knowledge Testing Center (KTC) (FAA AC 107-2A, p. 38).

According to 14 CFR § 107.160(a), a declaration of compliance is a submission made by an applicant for acceptance by the FAA to declare that a small unmanned aircraft is compliant with the requirements for Category 2 or Category 3 operations under Subpart D of Part 107. 

To be accepted, the declaration of compliance must include:
* The applicant's name, physical address, and email address (14 CFR § 107.160(a)(1)-(3));
* The small unmanned aircraft make, model name, and series (if applicable) (14 CFR § 107.160(a)(4));
* The small unmanned aircraft serial number or range of serial numbers subject to the declaration (14 CFR § 107.160(a)(5));
* Whether the declaration is an initial or an amended declaration, and if amended, the reason for re-submittal (14 CFR § 107.160(a)(6)-(7));
* The accepted means of compliance used to fulfill the requirements of § 107.120(a) (Category 2) or § 107.130(a) (Category 3), or both (14 CFR § 107.160(a)(8)); and
* A declaration stating that the applicant has demonstrated satisfaction of the criteria, verified the aircraft contains no safety defects, satisfied the labeling and remote pilot operating instruction requirements, and will allow the Administrator to inspect facilities, technical data, manufactured aircraft, and witness tests upon request (14 CFR § 107.160(a)(9)).

Prior to flight, the remote pilot in command must assess the operating environment, considering risks to persons and property in the immediate vicinity both on the surface and in the air. According to 14 CFR § 107.49(a), this assessment must include:

1. Local weather conditions;
2. Local airspace and any flight restrictions;
3. The location of persons and property on the surface; and
4. Other ground hazards.

Based on the provided regulations and advisory circular, an accident report required under 14 CFR § 107.9 must be submitted no later than 10 calendar days after the operation involving serious injury, loss of consciousness, or qualifying property damage (14 CFR § 107.9, FAA AC 107-2A). 

According to FAA AC 107-2A (p. 17), the report can be submitted as follows:
* **Manner of submission:** The report may be submitted to the appropriate FAA Regional Operations Center (ROC) electronically or by telephone. 
* **Electronic reporting:** Can be completed at [https://www.faa.gov/uas/](https://www.faa.gov/uas/).
* **By phone:** You may contact the appropriate FAA Regional Operations Center by telephone. 
* **Alternative offices:** Reports may also be made to the responsible Flight Standards office (refer to [https://www.faa.gov/about/office_org/field_offices/fsdo/](https://www.faa.gov/about/office_org/field_offices/fsdo/)).

**Information to include in the report:**
1. Small UAS remote PIC’s name and contact information (FAA AC 107-2A);
2. Small UAS remote PIC’s FAA airman certificate number (FAA AC 107-2A);
3. Small UAS registration number issued to the aircraft (FAA registration number) (FAA AC 107-2A);
4. Location of the accident (FAA AC 107-2A);
5. Date of the accident (FAA AC 107-2A);
6. Local time of the accident (FAA AC 107-2A);
7. Whether any serious injury or fatality occurred (FAA AC 107-2A);
8. Property damaged and extent of damage, if any or known (FAA AC 107-2A); and
9. Description of what happened (FAA AC 107-2A).
